## Methods
Substrates. For surface functionalization, and to serve as internal standard materials during elemental mapping, a stack of titanium-based layers ending in titanium oxide was deposited between the silicon wafer support and the zinc oxide layer. These titanium oxide substrate stacks were prepared by respectively sputtering titanium $\mathrm{.15nm},$ ), titanium nitride chemical vapour deposition $(140\mathrm{nm})$ and titanium oxide ALD $\mathrm{{'}40n m})$ on silicon wafers. Silicon dioxide reference substrates were prepared by wet oxidation of silicon wafers. Silicon pillars (staggered square array with pillar diameter of $2\upmu\mathrm{m}.$ pillar height $50\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and inter pillar distance of $2\upmu\mathrm{m}\dot{}$ ) were produced by means of reactive ion etching (Bosch process) using $600\mathrm{nm}$ silicon oxide patterned by photolithography as the hard mask.
Zinc oxide precursor film deposition. Zinc oxide precursor films were deposited using an ASM EmerALD module. ALD was conducted at $240^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using diethylzinc and oxygen plasma as precursors ( $500\mathrm{ms}$ pulse times and $2,000\mathrm{ms}$ purge times; 1.3 mbar reactor pressure). Zinc oxide thicknesses were verified by step-edge profilometry and ellipsometry. For lift-off patterning, zinc oxide sputtering was chosen instead of ALD because of its directionality; this deposition was performed using a NEXX Nimbus 310 module.
ZIF-8 vapour–solid reaction. ZIF-8 vapour–solid reactions were performed in three steps. First, the zinc-oxide-coated substrate and a closed reactor vessel containing a powder bed of 2-methylimidazole (HmIM) were preheated, separately, at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . In a second step, the substrate was suspended, top down, in the reactor vessel, $5\mathrm{cm}$ above the HmIM powder bed. The vessel was quickly closed and placed for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Last, the substrate was removed from the bottle while still at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and subsequently activated on a hot plate at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a nitrogen flow for $10\mathrm{min}$ . The HmIM powder bed was reused for all reactions.
X-ray diffraction. XRD patterns were collected from both the ZnO precursor and ZIF-8 films using a PANalytical X’Pert PRO Multi-Purpose Diffractometer, fitted with a Co target tube and operated at $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $40\mathrm{mA}$ . The incident beam was defined using a $1/8^{\circ}$ fixed divergence slit and $2.3^{\circ}$ Soller slits. In the diffracted beam, a graphite monochromator (used to minimize unwanted wavelengths, including the Co $\mathrm{K\upbeta}$ signal) was fitted in front of the small, linear position-sensitive detector. Data were collected between 6 and $75^{\circ}2\theta$ with a step size of $0.033^{\circ}2\theta$ using conventional Bragg–Brentano diffraction geometry52.
Infrared spectroscopy. ATR-FTIR spectra were recorded on a Varian 670 FTIR spectrometer coupled to a Varian 620 infrared microscope equipped with a slide-on Ge ATR tip.
Electron microscopy and elemental mapping. SEM images were recorded using a Philips XL30 FEG. The samples were sputter coated with $5\mathrm{nm}$ of Pt before the analysis. TEM samples were coated with a Pt or $\mathrm{Pt/Al}$ capping layer by sputtering. Cross-section specimens were prepared by focused ion beam lift-out using a FEI Nova600i dual beam system. TEM was executed using a FEI Tecnai F30 scanning microscope equipped with a FEG electron source operated at $300\mathrm{kV},$ an EDS and a HAADF detector. Average thickness estimations were obtained using the Fiji distribution53 of the ImageJ 1.48 software: the surface area of the cross-sections was first calculated and subsequently divided by the lateral length to obtain the average film thickness.
Atomic force microscopy. AFM topography images $(4\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ , $1,024\times1,024$ pixels, pixel size ${\sim}2\mathrm{nm}$ ) were recorded in intermittent contact mode using an Asylum Research Cypher ES AFM instrument. Silicon tips (AC160TS-R3; drive frequency $200{\mathrm{-}}400\mathrm{kHz},$ were used. Data analysis (first-order least-mean-square background correction, arithmetic $(R_{\mathrm{a}})$ and root-mean-squared $(R_{\mathrm{q}})$ surface roughness calculations) was performed using the SPIP 6.0.2 and Gwyddion 2.39 software54.
Time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry. TOF-SIMS analyses were measured with a TOF-SIMS IV instrument from ION-TOF GmbH. Positive ion profiles were measured in a dual beam configuration using a ${\mathrm{Bi}^{3+}}$ (25 kV) gun for analysis and a ${\mathrm{O}}^{2+}$ $500\mathrm{eV},$ ) gun for sputtering.
Krypton physisorption porosimetry. The porosimetry tests have been performed according to previous works55 and reproduced here for completeness. Krypton adsorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ were measured using a Micromeritics 3Flex 3500 gas physisorption instrument. The samples were degassed before measurement at $373\mathrm{K}$ under vacuum $(10^{-2}\mathrm{mbar})$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Gas uptake equilibria were measured between $10^{-5}$ and 2.15 mbar (deposition saturation pressure, $P_{\mathrm{0,solid}}$ ). As adsorption of liquid-like multilayers is assumed in the BET theory, the extrapolated condensation saturation pressure of the supercooled liquid $P_{\mathrm{0,liquid}}\sim3.3$ mbar) was used as $P_{0}$ for isotherm plotting and BET calculations. The BET method was applied in the region between 0.005 and $0.05P/P_{0}$ for the ZIF-8-coated samples
and in the region between 0.05 and $0.2P/P_{0}$ for a reference zinc-oxide-coated sample. A krypton adsorptive cross-sectional area of $0.210\mathrm{nm}^{2}$ was assumed for the BET calculations. The BET specific surface area of the samples was corrected for the area of the silicon array support and the sample tube by subtraction of the area of a reference measurement. The calculated surface area of ZIF-8 was obtained by Monte Carlo sampling using $\mathrm{Zeo++}$ (ref. 56). The krypton hard sphere radius at the triple point37, $185\mathrm{pm}$ , was used as an approximation for the krypton probe radius at $77\mathrm{K}$ . The single-pulse time-resolved experiment was conducted by pulsing a single gas dose to the samples (0.34 mbar, corresponding to $1.3\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ ) and monitoring the pressure in the sample tube decrease over time. The flat film was prepared using our previously reported melt transformation method ( $\operatorname{l}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ zinc oxide precursor film, $10\mathrm{min}$ synthesis time; ref. 27).
In situ powder X-ray diffraction. In situ PXRD experiments were performed on an INEL diffractometer utilizing a CPS120 curved position-sensitive detector, capable of collecting $120^{\circ}~2\theta$ of diffraction data simultaneously. The diffractometer was fitted with a Co long-line focus tube operated at $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $35\mathrm{mA}$ . A curved graphite incident beam monochromator was used, and the samples were studied in Debye–Scherrer transmission geometry52. Data were collected continuously in $30\mathrm{s}$ increments for the duration of the experiment. The samples were heated using a hot-air blower. The temperature was controlled by a thermocouple positioned between the nozzle of the hot-air blower and the sample capillary. The reaction mixture and glass wool were carefully mixed in a $50{:}50\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ ratio. The mixture was loaded into $1.5\mathrm{mm}$ quartz capillaries. The capillaries were sealed before the measurement in the case of the reaction without flow. For the flow-through experiments, the open-ended capillaries were mounted in a Norby cell57. The dry nitrogen flow was taken directly from the gas bottle through a rotameter. The humid gas flow was prepared by bubbling nitrogen through a saturated $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{MgCl_{2}}}$ salt solution, resulting in $33\%$ relative humidity at room temperature. The flow rate was in both cases kept constant at approximately $10\mathrm{ml}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . The XRD data were analysed using the Rietveld method58,59, as implemented in the Topas software package (Bruker AXS). Literature crystal structures were used for ZIF-8 (ref. 25) and LT-HmIM (ref. 60). The phase fractions were quantified using a modified version of the O’Connor and Raven external standard method61.
Lift-off patterning. Wafers for lift-off patterning were prepared as follows. Silicon wafers were dried on a hot plate at $190^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ and then coated with AZ positive tone resist. The $1.2\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ -thick resist layer was produced by spin coating the wafer with AZ5214E resist at $3,000{\mathrm{~r.p.m}}$ ., followed by soft baking on a hot plate at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $1\mathrm{min}$ . The photo resist was then exposed to ultraviolet through a soda-lime mask on a MJB4 (Suss MicroTec) and developed in AZ400K developer, 1:4 diluted with DI water. The wafers were subsequently washed with water and dried under nitrogen flow. Zinc oxide $(20\mathrm{nm})$ was deposited by reactive radiofrequency sputtering (zinc target, oxygen plasma atmosphere). The vapour–solid reaction was performed for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The photoresist lift-off was subsequently conducted by sonication of the wafers in acetone at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ .
PDMS pillars. PDMS pillars were made by means of soft lithography. A silicon mould was prepared using Bosch processing $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ diameter, ${5\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ pillar height and $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ inter pillar distance). The silicon mould was then vapour-treated with an anti-sticking layer (Trichloro(1H,1H,2H,2H-perfluorooctyl)silane). PDMS elastomer 184 from Dow Corning was prepared by mixing the base resin with its reticulating agent in 10:1 ratio and outgassed for $30\mathrm{min}$ in a vacuum jar; the mixture was then poured on the mould and outgassed for a further $30\mathrm{min}$ to provide a careful filling of the cavities. After curing at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ , $1\times1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ patches were cut with a razor blade and gently peeled off. The pillars were coated with $3\mathrm{nm}$ of zinc oxide by ALD and with ZIF-8 by vapour–solid reaction for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
